@page

<p>
    HTML says what is on the page. <strong>CSS</strong> (Cascading Style Sheets) says how it looks:
    colors, fonts, sizes, and spacing.
</p>

<h2>Parts of a CSS rule</h2>
<pre>
h1 {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 40px;
}
</pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>h1</code> is the <strong>selector</strong>. It picks which tags to style.</li>
    <li><code>color</code> and <code>font-size</code> are <strong>properties</strong>.</li>
    <li><code>blue</code> and <code>40px</code> are <strong>values</strong>.</li>
    <li>Each line ends with a semicolon, just like in C#.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Selectors</h2>
<pre>
/* Every paragraph */
p {
  line-height: 1.6;
}

/* Only tags with class="note" */
.note {
  background-color: lightyellow;
}

/* Links when the mouse is over them */
a:hover {
  color: red;
}
</pre>
<p>
    A selector with a dot, like <code>.note</code>, is a <strong>class selector</strong>. You add
    <code>class="note"</code> to a tag in your HTML and style it in your CSS. The yellow boxes in
    these lessons use the <code>note</code> class.
</p>

<h2>Colors</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Color names, like <code>red</code> or <code>blue</code></li>
    <li>Hex codes, like <code>#2c5aa0</code>. The pairs are how much red, green, and blue to mix.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The box model</h2>
<p>The browser treats every tag as a box with four layers:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Content</strong> - the text or picture</li>
    <li><strong>Padding</strong> - space inside the border</li>
    <li><strong>Border</strong> - a line around the box</li>
    <li><strong>Margin</strong> - space outside the border</li>
</ol>

<h2>Where the CSS is in this project</h2>
<p>
    All the styles for this website are in <code>wwwroot/css/site.css</code>. The layout file
    links to it, so every page gets the same look.
</p>

<p class="note">
    <strong>Try it:</strong> Open <code>wwwroot/css/site.css</code> and change
    <code>#2c5aa0</code> to <code>green</code> in the <code>header</code> rule. Save and watch the
    header change color.
</p>
